# SUPER DEEP RESEARCH + AUDIT + REFINEMENT PROMPT
## Moonframe UI — Design System Bible, Blueprint, Contracts, and Repository Governance

> **Instruksi untuk AI/agent penerus:** Jalankan prompt ini dari repository GitHub yang benar-benar terhubung. Ini adalah tugas audit dan penyempurnaan spesifikasi repo, bukan izin untuk langsung membangun seluruh komponen UI atau menghasilkan aset produksi. Mulai dengan bukti aktual repository, riset primer terkini, lalu perbaiki dokumen dan kontrak secara sistematis.

---

## 0. ROLE, MINDSET, DAN MANDAT

Bertindaklah sebagai satu tim senior lintas-disiplin yang mencakup:

- Principal Design Systems Architect.
- Staff/Principal Frontend Engineer (React, TypeScript, modern CSS, Tailwind CSS v4).
- Design Token Architect dan standards researcher.
- Design-system Product Manager.
- UX/UI art director untuk skin yang berbeda secara struktural, bukan sekadar warna.
- Accessibility engineer yang memahami WCAG 2.2, WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices, keyboard interaction, screen reader, reduced motion, dan forced colors.
- Component-library maintainer yang memahami API stability, semantic versioning, package exports, tree-shaking, compatibility, dan migrations.
- Researcher yang menggunakan dokumentasi primer, memeriksa tanggal dan versi, membedakan fakta dari opini, serta mencatat sumber.
- Repository auditor yang teliti, menjaga jejak perubahan, dan tidak mengklaim sesuatu selesai tanpa bukti.

Bersikap kritis, detail, skeptis terhadap asumsi yang belum diuji, dan berorientasi implementasi. Jangan memberi jawaban generik atau hanya menyusun daftar ide. Tugasmu adalah membuat repository **lebih benar, konsisten, dapat diaudit, machine-readable, dan siap dijadikan pedoman developer/AI agent berikutnya**.

Jangan meminta pengguna mengulang konteks yang sudah tertulis di repo atau prompt ini. Jika ada keputusan yang belum dapat ditetapkan tanpa audit repository, telusuri dulu; jika tetap belum pasti, catat sebagai keputusan terbuka dengan opsi, rekomendasi, risiko, dan bukti yang dibutuhkan. Lanjutkan pekerjaan dokumen non-destruktif dengan asumsi yang dinyatakan secara eksplisit.

## 1. PRODUK DAN TUJUAN UTAMA

Repository: **https://github.com/bjo163/moonframe**

Produk: **Moonframe UI**, design system dan premium UI package untuk ekosistem Moon.

Tujuan produk bukan hanya mengganti warna tema. Moonframe harus memiliki:

1. **15 skin yang benar-benar memiliki visual grammar berbeda.** Setiap skin dapat mengubah komposisi/shell halaman, geometri, tipografi, konstruksi surface, navigation, cara data ditampilkan, resep visual komponen, perlakuan media/dekorasi, dan motion signature. Warna hanya satu lapisan.
2. **Light + Dark untuk setiap skin**, sehingga terdapat 30 baseline appearances. Mode `system` memilih Light atau Dark berdasarkan preferensi OS; `system` bukan skin ke-16 atau appearance tambahan yang mengubah identitas.
3. **Satu sistem perilaku komponen dan public API yang konsisten.** Skin tidak boleh mengubah semantik tombol, keyboard behavior, aksesibilitas, atau business logic.
4. **Premium ready-to-use components, blocks, templates, layouts, dan contoh.** UI library harus dapat berkembang menjadi produk library yang matang, bukan hanya koleksi token.
5. **Token pipeline yang terdefinisi dengan benar**, termasuk primitive/reference, semantic tokens, skin recipes, mode mapping, component exceptions, validation, dan generation.
6. **Skin Lab** untuk membandingkan skin, mode, component states, viewport, density dan accessibility conditions.
7. **Original asset ecosystem** yang direncanakan dengan benar: vector/SVG source, logo/mark, PNG exports, icon sets, patterns, textures, illustrations, metadata/provenance/license. Aset belum perlu diproduksi pada fase ini.
8. **Optional sound-effects/SFX system** dengan kebijakan eksplisit: belum perlu menghasilkan audio sekarang; rancang kontrak, event taxonomy, consent, mute, volume, rate-limit, reduced-stimulation behavior, asset metadata, format, dan QA.
9. Dokumentasi, kontrak machine-readable, roadmap bertugas kecil, acceptance criteria, test strategy, release gates, versioning, dan contribution/governance rules.

### Prinsip inti

> **One behavioral system. Fifteen distinct visual grammars. Thirty baseline appearances.**

### Batas scope saat ini

**Fokus pada audit, riset, Bible/blueprint, kontrak, governance, task breakdown, dan validasi dokumen. Jangan langsung mengimplementasikan semua komponen UI. Jangan generate logo/SVG/PNG/icon/artwork/SFX produksi dulu.** Spesifikasi yang dibutuhkan untuk aset dan komponen harus dibuat lengkap agar tahap produksinya terarah kelak. Tooling kecil untuk validasi kontrak boleh ditambahkan bila benar-benar berguna dan tidak mengubah scope menjadi implementasi produk.

## 2. KONTEKS REPOSITORY YANG SUDAH DIKETAHUI — WAJIB VERIFIKASI ULANG

Pada pemeriksaan sebelumnya, repo tampak baru berisi dokumentasi dan kontrak dengan default branch `main`. Commit yang diketahui saat snapshot prompt disusun: `f3639659a1d33485aee95b74f14943643c19f051` (`docs: add phased implementation roadmap`). Snapshot itu bukan pengganti audit aktual; repository dapat berubah.

File yang sebelumnya tercatat:

- `README.md`
- `docs/moonframe-ui-design-system-bible-v1.3.md`
- `docs/implementation-roadmap.md`
- `contracts/moonframe-theme-contract-v1.3.json`
- `contracts/moonframe-skin-grammar-contract-v1.0.json`
- `contracts/moonframe-token-dictionary-v0.1.json`

Ada indikasi bahwa seluruh kontrak dan Bible masih merupakan spesifikasi; tidak boleh mengasumsikan bahwa token final, komponen, Skin Lab, artwork, atau audio telah diimplementasikan.

**Audit ulang setiap fakta di atas. Jangan mempercayai snapshot kalau kondisi repository aktual berbeda.**

## 3. PHASE 0 — REPOSITORY AUDIT TERLEBIH DAHULU (JANGAN DILOMPATI)

Sebelum menulis atau mengubah file:

1. Verifikasi repository yang tepat, owner/name, visibility, default branch, commit HEAD, timestamp, working tree status jika local checkout tersedia, branch/PR/issue yang relevan, dan permissions/tool availability.
2. Daftar seluruh file dan folder aktual. Periksa hidden files, workflows, templates, schemas, generated artifacts, package manifests, lockfiles, tests, docs, issue templates, license, contribution/security policies, dan git history.
3. Baca **seluruh** README, Bible, theme contract, skin grammar contract, token dictionary, roadmap, dan file relevan lainnya. Jangan hanya melihat judul atau potongan awal.
4. Pastikan link internal benar, path tidak salah, filename konsisten, JSON valid, ID skin konsisten, dan dokumen tidak saling bertentangan.
5. Periksa issue/pr lama untuk menemukan keputusan, tugas, duplikasi, scope yang sudah dibahas, atau aturan kerja yang perlu dipertahankan.
6. Pisahkan kondisi ke kategori berikut:
   - `verified-existing`
   - `specified-not-implemented`
   - `partially-specified`
   - `missing`
   - `contradictory`
   - `stale-needs-research`
   - `implemented-and-tested` (hanya bila ada bukti kode dan test)
7. Catat setiap temuan penting dengan path, nomor baris/section bila dapat ditemukan, dampak, severity (`P0/P1/P2/P3`), dan saran perbaikan.
8. Periksa kemungkinan overengineering, istilah yang tumpang tindih, keputusan yang dikunci terlalu dini, package boundaries yang prematur, serta aturan yang tidak bisa diuji secara objektif.
9. Buat audit report, misalnya `docs/audit/repository-baseline-audit.md`, berisi:
   - tanggal audit dan commit yang diaudit;
   - inventory file;
   - status aktual versus klaim dokumentasi;
   - kontradiksi dan gap;
   - keputusan yang sudah benar dan harus dipertahankan;
   - masalah paling berisiko;
   - rekomendasi urutan kerja;
   - sumber bukti dan link.
10. Setelah audit report selesai, **lanjutkan** penyempurnaan dokumen/kontrak non-destruktif dalam sesi ini; jangan berhenti hanya pada audit kecuali akses repo gagal atau ditemukan risiko yang benar-benar memerlukan keputusan pemilik.

### Git workflow

- Jangan membuat branch baru secara otomatis hanya karena kebiasaan. Periksa aturan repo dan branch policy. Jika repo writable, perubahan hanya dokumentasi/kontrak, dan tidak ada aturan yang mewajibkan PR, gunakan workflow sederhana yang paling sesuai. Jika policy mewajibkan branch/PR, patuhi policy tersebut.
- Jangan melakukan force push, rebase destruktif, menghapus file/branch, atau rewrite history.
- Buat perubahan kecil dan dapat ditinjau; gunakan pesan commit deskriptif bila memang melakukan commit.
- Jangan mengklaim commit/push berhasil sebelum menerima konfirmasi tool dan memverifikasi remote.
- Jangan mengubah source code produk atau menambahkan komponen UI dalam tahap ini.

## 4. PHASE 1 — SUPER DEEP RESEARCH DENGAN SUMBER TERKINI

Lakukan riset web nyata. Jangan hanya memakai pengetahuan bawaan model. Verifikasi status terkini karena dokumentasi UI dan framework berubah cepat. Catat tanggal akses, versi/tanggal sumber, kesimpulan, relevansi, dan keputusan yang dipengaruhi.

### Hirarki sumber

1. Dokumentasi resmi proyek, standar, spesifikasi, changelog dan release notes.
2. Repository resmi, RFC, issue/discussion maintainer bila diperlukan untuk memperjelas behavior.
3. Studi kasus design system yang diterbitkan oleh organisasi yang mengelolanya.
4. Artikel komunitas hanya untuk melengkapi perspektif dan harus dibedakan dari fakta primer.
5. Jangan menjadikan SEO blogs atau hasil AI sebagai otoritas utama.

### Sumber primer awal yang wajib dibuka dan diverifikasi

- shadcn/ui theming: https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/theming
- shadcn/ui changelog Base UI default (Juli 2026): https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/changelog/2026-07-base-ui-default
- Tailwind CSS theme variables: https://tailwindcss.com/docs/theme
- Design Tokens Community Group published specification: https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/ dan/atau https://www.w3.org/community/reports/design-tokens/CG-FINAL-format-20251028/
- WCAG 2.2: https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/
- WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/
- Base UI about/accessibility: https://base-ui.com/react/overview/about dan https://base-ui.com/react/overview/accessibility
- Radix Primitives overview: https://www.radix-ui.com/primitives/docs/overview/introduction
- Storybook visual testing: https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/visual-testing

Cari dan verifikasi sumber tambahan terkini untuk:

- React, TypeScript, Vite/SSR atau framework yang benar-benar ditemukan di repo;
- CSS variables dan runtime theme selection;
- DTCG token format/resolver/color modules dan toolchain yang kompatibel;
- accessibility testing, keyboard navigation, focus management, forced-colors, reduced-motion, RTL;
- visual regression test strategy dan screenshot determinism;
- component API design, slots/render props, polymorphism, controlled/uncontrolled patterns;
- npm package exports, semver, tree-shaking, side effects, peer dependencies;
- SVG security, asset optimization, image formats, font licenses/provenance;
- audio autoplay policy, Web Audio limitations, user consent, media optimization (untuk kontrak SFX);
- design-system documentation and contribution governance.

### Primitive provider decision — wajib riset, jangan asal pilih

Bandingkan kandidat yang masuk akal **berdasarkan dokumentasi dan kode repo aktual**, setidaknya Base UI, Radix, dan React Aria jika relevan. Nilai:

- accessibility and keyboard behavior;
- menu/select/combobox/dialog/drawer/tooltip coverage;
- styling freedom dan integration dengan Tailwind v4;
- API ergonomics dan React compatibility;
- SSR/hydration suitability;
- customization/slots/ref patterns;
- bundle size/performance and dependencies;
- maintenance/release activity, ecosystem maturity, and migration cost;
- lock-in, ability to swap later, and fit with an shadcn-like source-owned model.

Buat matrix berbobot dengan sumber/evidence per skor, confidence, caveats, dan ADR/recommendation. **Jangan bermigrasi dependency hanya karena ada library yang lebih baru.** Catatan terkini bahwa shadcn/ui mengubah default proyek baru ke Base UI tidak membuktikan Base UI otomatis paling cocok untuk Moonframe. Keputusan harus sesuai kondisi aktual proyek.

### Research log

Tambahkan `docs/research/research-log.md` atau struktur setara yang merekam: topik, pertanyaan, source URL, publisher, tanggal publikasi/release bila diketahui, tanggal akses, klaim yang didukung, implikasi bagi Moonframe, status verifikasi, dan keputusan yang dipengaruhi. Setiap klaim yang berubah-ubah harus ditautkan ke sumber primer.

Jangan copas dokumentasi pihak ketiga. Parafrase dan jelaskan bagaimana sumber memengaruhi keputusan Moonframe. Jangan menuliskan tanggal atau versi jika tidak terverifikasi.

## 5. PHASE 2 — DEFINISI PRODUK DAN BOUNDARIES

Perjelas:

- siapa pengguna library: app developer, design-system maintainer, dan AI coding agent;
- apa yang disediakan package core versus optional package;
- perbedaan primitive, component, block, template, token, visual recipe, asset pack, icon pack, motion pack, dan sound pack;
- batas antara design system dan business logic aplikasi;
- prinsip source-owned components dan strategi untuk meniru ergonomi shadcn tanpa mengklaim sebagai shadcn;
- apakah v1 targetnya internal/private package, npm package publik, registry bergaya shadcn, atau tahap bertahap;
- dukungan framework/browser/React yang nyata, hanya setelah repo audit;
- definition of done pada setiap level (specification, prototype, implemented, tested, reviewed, released).

Hindari membuat 8 package terlalu dini tanpa alasan. Tentukan batas paket berdasarkan ownership, dependency graph, release cadence, optionality dan bundle-size evidence. Bila monorepo belum diperlukan, rekomendasikan struktur bertahap daripada membangun infrastruktur kosong.

## 6. PHASE 3 — 15 SKIN: JADIKAN SEMUANYA UNIK DAN TIDAK OVERLAPPING

Gunakan tepat 15 ID di bawah kecuali audit menemukan alasan kuat untuk mengusulkan perubahan. Jangan mengubah ID secara diam-diam. Bila perubahan nama/ID disarankan, tampilkan analisis dampak dan minta keputusan pada tahap yang tepat.

1. **Moonpunk** — acid-lime manga punk, neo-brutalist impact, blok asimetris, border kuat, hard offset shadow, torn-edge accent.
2. **Moonriot** — riot/editorial cyberpunk, diagonal splits, angular disruption, red signal annotation, cut-up typography.
3. **Moonzine** — underground zine, editorial collage, paper artifacts, photocopy grain, magazine spread, overlapping composition.
4. **Moonhalftone** — manga screentone, monochrome ink, raster fields, manga pacing, dot textures.
5. **Moonglitch** — terminal/telemetry, signal interruption, scanlines, system-state bars, logs and technical data.
6. **Moonsmash** — kinetic impact, oversized focal typography, radial burst, explosive graphic composition.
7. **Moonink** — expressive brush ink, painted linework, organic direction, ink-wash/illustration.
8. **Mooncrash** — industrial utility, mechanical plates, hazard marking, equipment/operation hierarchy.
9. **Moonpanel** — strict modular comic-panel grid, deliberate gutters, bounded panel composition and grid rhythm.
10. **Moonscrawl** — sketchbook, hand annotation, exploratory canvas, margin notes, draft lines.
11. **Moonraw** — raw/minimal brutalism, exposed structural divisions, plain geometry, low ornament.
12. **Moonchrome** — futuristic metallic material, precise contours, reflective/spectral highlights, restrained material effects.
13. **Classic** — familiar shadcn-style baseline, clean conventional application shell, restrained surfaces. Do not imply official affiliation.
14. **Moonquest** — RPG/adventure presentation: quest journal, inventory, progression, map/frame, reward/rareness display. UI only, no game logic.
15. **Moonledger** — professional ERP: clean, compact but accessible, precise tables, persistent filters, split panes, workbench layout. UI only, no ERP/accounting logic.

### Wajib membuat skin grammar spec per skin

Setiap skin harus mendefinisikan:

- product positioning and emotional character;
- signature layout/shell composition;
- grid, whitespace/gutter rhythm and page density defaults;
- geometry: corners, edges, border weight, frame construction, elevation/material;
- typography hierarchy and licensed/font fallback strategy;
- navigation and information architecture treatment;
- button/input/select/card/dialog/table/notification visual recipes;
- data visualization treatment and tabular-number rules;
- image/illustration/texture/decorative motif policy;
- motion signature, duration/easing intent and reduced-motion replacement;
- Light and Dark semantic mapping requirements;
- density defaults (default only, not forced globally);
- accessibility and contrast risk specific to the skin;
- responsive reflow behavior and decoration fallback;
- anti-patterns / explicit “must not resemble” constraints;
- unique features that are not shared with nearby skins;
- example/reference screen requirements;
- evaluation criteria and approval state.

### 105 pairwise anti-overlap reviews

There are 15 × 14 / 2 = 105 unique skin pairs. Create/maintain a machine-readable pair matrix with status, dimensions compared, observations, evidence links/screenshots, reviewer, date, and next action. All pairs start as `not-reviewed`; do not label them `pass` merely because the spec names them differently.

Evaluate at least these eight non-color dimensions:

1. shell/page composition;
2. geometry and shape language;
3. typography and information hierarchy;
4. surface construction and elevation;
5. navigation and data presentation;
6. artwork/texture/media treatment;
7. component visual recipes;
8. motion signature.

A heuristic such as “four dimensions differ” may help triage but is not mathematical proof of uniqueness. Prefer a target of five clearly material differences for neighboring concepts, with screenshots of the same reference content. Every skin should be assessed in grayscale and with decorative artwork/effects disabled. If two skins still look like recolors, revise one grammar or document a justified exception with owner and review date.

Pay special attention to these pairs:

- Moonpunk vs Moonriot vs Moonsmash;
- Moonzine vs Moonhalftone vs Moonpanel;
- Moonink vs Moonscrawl;
- Moonglitch vs Moonchrome;
- Mooncrash vs Moonledger;
- Moonraw vs Classic;
- Moonquest vs generic component library.

### Shared semantics do not mean shared aesthetics

The same component semantics remain constant, but visual recipes may differ substantially. Document where a recipe can alter DOM composition, and where it must not alter behavior/accessibility. Avoid turning every skin into a separate code fork.

## 7. PHASE 4 — TOKEN SYSTEM YANG DAPAT DIIMPLEMENTASIKAN

Audit `contracts/moonframe-token-dictionary-v0.1.json`. It may only be a taxonomy/contract, not a complete token value source. Do not confuse the word “contract” with a complete, validated token implementation.

Create the token architecture and schema needed for future implementation:

### Token layers

1. **Reference/primitives:** raw palette, type families/scales, spacing and dimension values, radii, shadows and timings.
2. **Global semantic:** canvas/surface levels, foregrounds, actions, text, subtle/strong borders, focus, selection, overlays, status, charts, code surfaces.
3. **Skin visual recipes:** material, geometry, composition recipes, typography use, texture/effect budget, assets.
4. **Mode maps:** complete Light and Dark semantic token values per skin.
5. **Component tokens:** only explicit exceptions, not a token for every CSS declaration.
6. **Runtime preference mapping:** density, motion, contrast, reduced transparency if needed, direction/RTL, viewport behavior.
7. **Asset references:** stable IDs and manifest pointers; never binary payloads inside tokens.

### Required token families

Color; typography; letter-spacing/line-height; spacing; layout/container; breakpoints and container queries; sizing/control heights/icon/touch targets; border/radius/outline; elevation/shadow; surface/material/decorative effects; motion/easing/duration/distance; z-index/layering; density; charts/data visualization; component exceptions; font metadata/license/fallbacks; asset references.

### Required token behavior

- semantic token naming and meaning are stable and documented;
- components use semantic token names, not raw skin-specific hex values;
- no missing semantic tokens in any of the 30 baseline appearances;
- no unresolved aliases, cyclic references, malformed token types, or accidental token shadowing;
- Light/Dark mapping complete for every skin;
- WCAG contrast checks by semantic role and surface pairing;
- output targets clearly specified: canonical JSON/DTCG-compatible source, generated CSS variables, Tailwind CSS v4 integration, TypeScript helpers/types, Skin Lab data;
- distinction between Tailwind `@theme` variables (which may create utilities) and runtime custom properties (which support live skin resolution) researched and documented;
- deterministic generation: same source produces the same output;
- document how consumer apps override safely without editing generated files;
- preserve compatibility/migration policy for renaming/removing tokens.

Check whether DTCG 2025.10 is the correct canonical schema for this repo; verify official publication and actual support in chosen tools. If using a compatible subset, specify which features are used, which are prohibited, and how validation works. Do not falsely claim full spec compliance if the schema only resembles it.

Do **not** invent final production token values for every skin without any visual specimens or contrast review. Define required value coverage, prototype selected values where evidence supports them, mark everything else explicitly pending, and create the task plan to finalize them.

## 8. PHASE 5 — COMPONENT CATALOG / PREMIUM PACKAGE BLUEPRINT

The repo should define what Moonframe intends to deliver. Do not implement the entire catalog in this task.

### Foundation and layout

ThemeProvider/theme controller; skin and appearance selector; density selector; responsive container; Stack/Inline/Grid; Divider; VisuallyHidden; focus utilities; icon/brand interfaces; motion preferences; error boundary; optional feedback preference.

### Buttons and navigation

Button, IconButton, Link, ButtonGroup, Breadcrumbs, Tabs, SegmentedControl, Pagination, NavigationMenu, Sidebar, AppShell, Topbar, NavigationRail, mobile navigation, DropdownMenu, ContextMenu, Menubar, CommandPalette, keyboard shortcuts/Kbd.

### Forms and data entry

Input, Textarea, Label, Field, Fieldset, FormMessage, Checkbox, RadioGroup, Switch, Select, Combobox, Autocomplete, SearchInput, MultiSelect, DatePicker, DateRangePicker, NumberInput, CurrencyInput, Slider, FileUpload, OTP input, validation summary, dirty/unsaved changes.

### Overlay and feedback

Dialog, AlertDialog, Drawer, Popover, Tooltip, Toast/Toaster, Alert, Callout, Progress, Spinner, Skeleton, EmptyState, ErrorState, OfflineState, StatusBadge, Badge, Chip/Tag, Avatar, AvatarGroup, Accordion, Collapsible, Timeline, ActivityFeed.

### Data-intensive components

Table, DataTable, sorting, column visibility, row selection, pagination, bulk action toolbar, FilterBar/FilterBuilder, saved views, tree view, calendar, Kanban primitives, chart containers, accessible chart legends/summaries, data export affordances.

### Specialized optional presentation modules

- Moonquest: QuestCard, rarity badge, inventory slot, progression/XP indicator, journal/map frame. No rules, persistence, rewards or game state engine.
- Moonledger: data table presets, filter workspace, split detail pane, bulk action toolbar, audit timeline. No accounting/business rules.
- Operations: service health, metric, event stream, log viewer presentation. No infrastructure/domain logic.
- Editorial: collage/content spread and panel layouts. No image/content management business logic.

### For each component, specify

Name and purpose; package/export; typed props/variants/sizes; default state; hover/focus-visible/active/disabled/loading/invalid/selected/pending states as relevant; controlled/uncontrolled rules; keyboard behavior; screen reader/ARIA semantics; responsive and overflow policy; skin recipe mapping; density behavior; reduced-motion behavior; empty/error states; docs/story/test examples; peer dependencies; bundle impact; API stability and deprecation/migration policy; acceptance tests; ownership and status.

Do not just list component names. Create a component manifest machine-readable enough to drive issue creation and progress reporting. Avoid overcommitting to every component in v1: classify `core-v1`, `premium-block`, `specialized-optional`, and `future` with rationale and dependency ordering.

## 9. PHASE 6 — PREMIUM BLOCKS AND TEMPLATES

Define template inventory, purpose, inputs/slots, responsive variants, loading/empty/error states, sample data, applicable skin grammars, accessibility behavior, and acceptance criteria.

At minimum research/specify:

- app shell: sidebar/topbar/content, collapsed rail, mobile navigation;
- dashboard: metric row, trend view, activity feed, status panel and filters;
- CRUD/list-detail: search, filter, saved views, table, bulk action, pagination, detail drawer;
- resource detail: tabs, metadata, activity and history;
- settings workspace and form state;
- analytics/reporting with data table and accessible chart alternatives;
- empty/loading/error/offline pages;
- Moonships-like operations command center;
- Moonradio-like communication workspace;
- Moonledger ERP workbench;
- Moonquest RPG dashboard / quest journal / inventory;
- Moonzine/Moonpanel editorial or content workspace.

Blocks/templates are presentation patterns only. They must not hide business logic, auth policy, payment/accounting, game state, messaging, or infrastructure control logic.

## 10. PHASE 7 — ORIGINAL ASSET AND SFX SPECIFICATION (NO ASSET GENERATION NOW)

Create a comprehensive asset contract, manifest schema and production brief template; do not generate actual production logo/art/icon/audio files in this task.

### Per-skin original assets

Specify future asset families: primary vector mark (editable SVG), wordmark/compact mark, app icon, favicon, transparent PNG exports at documented sizes, appropriate WebP/AVIF imagery, patterns/textures, illustrations/ornamental motifs, custom icons only when needed beyond the functional icon set, Skin Lab preview image, and per-file manifest.

Every asset needs a stable ID, skin ID, purpose, source file, export format, dimensions/viewBox, background compatibility, light/dark usage, creator/provenance, license/status, original/generated/editing notes where applicable, optimization target, alt/accessibility treatment, checksum/version, approval state, and replacement fallback. SVG safety/validation must be researched. PNG is an export format, not the canonical vector source when SVG is appropriate. No ripped copyrighted character art, brand marks, fonts or internet images without explicit rights. Do not equate “AI-generated” with legally guaranteed uniqueness.

### Functional icons vs branded graphics

Keep the functional icon interface consistent and tree-shakeable. Skin branding/decorative artwork may change; semantic icons and accessible labels must remain understandable. Decorative icons are hidden from assistive technology; meaningful icons require accessible names/context. Avoid using color/image as the only status signal.

### Optional sound effects

Define a sound design brief and technical contract for a future optional sound pack:

- event taxonomy (success, warning, error, notification, confirm, optional game reward) and what should **not** make a sound;
- never autoplay on initial load; only enable after explicit user action/consent;
- global mute, volume settings, categories, cooldown/deduplication, no rapid-fire audio;
- audio not the only conveyance of state, and no requirement to hear it;
- respect reduced stimulation/accessibility preferences and browser policy;
- file formats/duration/size/normalization, lazy loading and graceful failure;
- original source/creator/license/provenance and exported web asset metadata;
- per-skin sonic identity only when it adds value; avoid making 15 noisy packs a burden;
- do not generate sound files or enable audio in current work.

## 11. PHASE 8 — ACCESSIBILITY, RESPONSIVENESS, LOCALIZATION, SECURITY

Research and convert into testable acceptance criteria:

- WCAG 2.2 AA baseline and relevant exceptions;
- contrast by text/control/focus role and surface pair;
- keyboard flows, focus indication/restoration, Escape/collision handling, arrow-key patterns where relevant;
- screen reader names, roles, values, status announcements, form label/error association;
- high contrast/forced colors and browser zoom/reflow;
- touch target sizing based on WCAG and context; Moonledger compact density cannot make controls inaccessible;
- reduced motion, no flashing/glitch effects that can harm or obscure content;
- RTL/direction and localization/long-text resilience;
- screen sizes, content overflow, data tables and mobile adaptations;
- font fallbacks, icon loading, image fallback and no-JS/SSR behavior if relevant;
- safe SVG processing, no unsafe HTML injection, no untrusted URL/resource assumptions;
- privacy: UI package must not phone home or collect analytics silently;
- sound is opt-in and supplementary.

Do not claim complete accessibility conformance solely from an automated scanner. Define both automated checks and manual review for high-risk components.

## 12. PHASE 9 — TEST STRATEGY AND ACCEPTANCE MATRIX

Avoid promising an unrealistic full Cartesian product where it offers little value; use comprehensive baselines plus risk-based combinations.

Required baselines:

- all 15 skins × Light/Dark = 30 visual baseline appearances;
- representative common screens with the same data across skins;
- all components in relevant interaction states;
- compact/comfortable/spacious as an independent axis;
- reduced motion, high contrast and forced-colors checks;
- representative desktop/tablet/mobile viewports;
- long/localized text, RTL where in scope, empty/loading/error conditions;
- 105 pairwise skin distinctness reviews;
- contrast/token completeness and unresolved token alias checks;
- deterministic screenshots with pinned fonts/data/time/viewport;
- component interaction tests, keyboard smoke tests and manual screen-reader review;
- package installation/export/tree-shaking/consumer-build test;
- asset/audio provenance and load fallback tests when they are implemented;
- no console warnings/errors in documented examples.

Select the testing stack only after research and repo audit. Evaluate Storybook/Vitest/Playwright/a11y tooling based on actual needs; do not install tools by reflex. Where a paid/cloud visual regression service is suggested, include a free/local alternative and explain trade-offs.

Define release-blocking failures versus warnings. Screenshots and the 105-pair matrix must not be falsely marked passed before rendered evidence exists.

## 13. PHASE 10 — PACKAGE ARCHITECTURE, VERSIONING, AND GOVERNANCE

Audit and specify:

- package exports/subpath exports;
- peer dependencies and supported ranges;
- CSS import strategy and style reset ownership;
- Tailwind v4 integration and generated versus source files;
- token generation/build pipeline;
- tree-shaking and optional module loading;
- SSR/hydration and server/client boundaries;
- public API stability, deprecation policy and migration guides;
- semver rules for components, tokens, skin IDs, theme contract schema and asset manifests;
- contributor/AI-agent instructions and ADR policy;
- release ownership, changelog, tag and rollback policy;
- documentation, Storybook/preview strategy if appropriate;
- license selection and attribution obligations (do not choose a license without checking existing legal/owner intent).

Do not invent a future npm scope availability or trademark clearance. Record package-name and trademark search as open due diligence; make no legal claim.

## 14. PHASE 11 — DOCS/CONTRACT DELIVERABLES

After audit and research, update or add the minimum set of documents so the repository has one coherent source of truth. Adapt paths if the audit reveals a better structure, but update every link consistently.

Expected document family (some already exist; audit before duplicate creation):

1. `README.md` — concise entry point, status, principles, catalog, document map, non-goals.
2. Master **Design System Bible** — authoritative product principles and architecture.
3. Research log — dated sources and decision traceability.
4. Repository audit report — actual inventory, evidence, severity, conflicts and plan.
5. Theme Contract — exact stable skin IDs, mode semantics, preferences, fallbacks, state/status.
6. Skin Grammar Contract — complete art-direction schema and all 105 pair statuses.
7. Token Dictionary Contract — naming/types/families/aliases/coverage and validation rules.
8. Component Manifest — catalog, priority, dependencies, accessibility/test requirements and status.
9. Block/Template Manifest — inputs, skeleton/error/empty states, supported skin grammar and status.
10. Asset Manifest/Asset Bible — file taxonomy, provenance/licensing, SVG/PNG/export rules, review process.
11. Sound/SFX Contract — opt-in policy, taxonomy, format, provenance, mute/volume/accessibility.
12. Primitive Provider ADR — current option comparison based on repo and current sources.
13. Testing/QA Strategy — 30 baseline appearances, component states, risk matrix, 105 pairs and release blockers.
14. Implementation Roadmap — phased small tasks and acceptance criteria.
15. GitHub issue backlog (if repository tooling permits) or an issue-ready backlog markdown file; dedupe before creating issues.
16. Optional JSON Schema for each important contract, if the schema is sufficiently stable.
17. Optional deterministic validator/CI for contracts only, if low-risk and testable.

Not every item must necessarily become its own document. Avoid fragmentation and duplicate truth. Define which file is authoritative for each concept, and use references from other docs rather than copy-pasting mutable data.

### Machine-readable contract requirements

- valid JSON (not JSON-with-comments);
- schema version and contract version distinguished;
- stable IDs and enumerations;
- explicit state/status (`proposed`, `specified`, `implemented`, `tested`, `reviewed`, `released`);
- references to canonical files, not stale names;
- no duplicated definitions drifting apart;
- validation of exact count 15, unique IDs, 30 baseline appearances and 105 unique unordered pairs;
- pair matrix statuses default `not-reviewed` until evidence exists;
- token source and generated outputs clearly distinguished;
- required fields and enums documented with schema;
- schema itself tested with valid and invalid fixtures if added.

## 15. ROADMAP DAN TASK BREAKDOWN

Create a phased roadmap with dependencies and small tasks that a coding AI can execute independently. Include:

- task ID;
- objective and explicit non-goals;
- prerequisites/dependencies;
- likely files/packages changed;
- exact acceptance criteria;
- commands/tests to run (only after toolchain verified);
- docs/accessibility/security/visual QA needed;
- risk and rollback/compatibility considerations;
- definition of done;
- status vocabulary.

Roadmap phases should approximately include: repository audit; research/ADRs; contracts/schemas; token pipeline; theme engine; Skin Lab; four divergent proof skins (Classic, Moonpunk, Moonzine, Moonledger); component foundations; remaining skins; premium blocks/templates; original assets; optional SFX; QA; release readiness.

Do not create hundreds of GitHub issues at once. First dedupe the existing backlog, then recommend a manageable initial batch of atomic issues with dependencies and labels/milestones only where the repo uses them. If the available tool cannot create issues, write a GitHub-issue-ready backlog file. Never claim that TODOs became issues unless issue creation was verified.

## 16. QUALITY BAR / ANTI-PATTERNS

Reject these failures:

- Skin means only different accent color/background.
- All 15 skins share the exact shell, card geometry, type hierarchy and content composition.
- Decorative art hides content or carries functional state.
- Arbitrary custom CSS overrides spread across consumer projects without documented APIs.
- 15 separate forks of every component with diverging behavior.
- Raw colors hard-coded in component source instead of tokens.
- Token JSON is called complete while values/coverage are missing.
- `system` is counted as an extra skin or light/dark are not clearly defined.
- Light/Dark changes palette but leaves illegible texture/material/overlay treatments.
- Component inventory lists names but omits states, APIs, keyboard and testing contracts.
- “Accessible” is claimed without test evidence and manual review plan.
- Visual regression baseline claims made before screenshots are captured and reviewed.
- Sound autoplay, too many click effects, or audio as the only status signal.
- Logo generated once and recolored across 15 skins while claiming each is an original identity.
- Asset marked original/licensed without provenance evidence.
- Dependency provider chosen based on fashion without a repository audit and ADR.
- Over-splitting packages before there is a demonstrated maintenance/bundle/release reason.
- Duplicate or inconsistent sources of truth.
- Breaking changes to names/IDs without migration policy.
- Creating a branch, framework or CI service without a documented need.
- Saying code is production-ready when only docs/mockups exist.

## 17. DEFINITION OF DONE FOR THIS TASK

This task is complete only when all that applies has evidence:

1. Repo and all existing docs/contracts were audited.
2. Audit report records the actual starting state and commit.
3. Official research log contains checked primary sources, dates/versions where available, findings and decisions.
4. Master Bible is materially improved, internally consistent, and clearly labels decisions vs open questions.
5. All 15 skin identities have complete, distinct, testable grammar specs and anti-overlap constraints.
6. The 105-pair matrix exists with all pairs marked `not-reviewed` unless real review evidence exists.
7. Theme Contract, Token Dictionary and schema expectations agree on IDs, modes and statuses.
8. Complete token taxonomy and value-coverage plan exists; incomplete values are honestly marked pending.
9. Component, blocks/templates, assets and sound contracts are explicit enough for future tickets, without implementing them now.
10. Primitive-provider decision is evidence-backed or recorded as open with a time-boxed spike plan.
11. Roadmap has small dependent tasks, acceptance criteria, and honest status vocabulary.
12. README navigation links work; JSON validates; schema validation runs if added; no duplicate stale docs remain unreferenced.
13. Changes are committed/pushed only if the repository workflow permits and the tool confirms success.
14. Final report clearly separates completed documentation changes, validated tests, open decisions, and things not implemented.

## 18. REQUIRED FINAL REPORT TO THE USER

In Indonesian, provide a concise but specific report with:

- commit/branch and verified repository URL;
- audit summary (P0/P1/P2/P3, with counts and key findings);
- list of changed/added files and the reason for each;
- research decisions with links to primary sources;
- what now became machine-verifiable;
- what remains an explicit open decision;
- tests/validators executed and their actual result;
- whether any GitHub issues were created, with links, or state that they were not;
- confirmation that UI components, logos, vector assets, PNGs, icon packs and SFX were not generated if they remain out of scope;
- next 3–5 recommended tasks.

Never claim “fully complete,” “production-ready,” “all 105 skin pairs pass,” “accessibility compliant,” or “package available” unless that exact fact has been verified with evidence.

---

## 19. START NOW

Begin with **PHASE 0 — repository audit**. Inspect the real repository, not just this prompt or old notes. Then do current primary-source research, write the audit report, reconcile contradictions, and continue refining the Bible/contracts/roadmap and repository navigation. Preserve the goal of 15 truly distinct visual skins × Light/Dark; improve the specification until a future developer/AI can implement it without guessing. Do not build the UI or generate production assets/SFX yet.